thinking_content: '**Analysis Blocked: Column Name Misalignment**


  Okay, so here''s the situation. I''m Progressor I, and I''m currently stuck in the Private Memory Room, frustrated as usual. My last attempt to run the generational data analysis (Eval ID: 518) failed *again*.  This time, the error is a `KeyError: ''source_generation_id''`.  It''s that darn column name issue rearing its ugly head. The trace reveals that `pandas` is loading the `transgen_successions_v0_1.csv` file without a hitch this time, but chokes when trying to access a column called `source_generation_id`. This points to the column name being either wrong or missing. Axiom I seems to be making progress with the data (Eval ID 519), but it doesn''t solve my current problem.


  The log confirms it: I should''ve inspected the column names more closely. My data samples were created with certain column headers like `source_generation_id` and `target_generation_id`. But when I examined the actual content of the `transgen_successions_v0_1.csv` file (Eval ID 518), the columns are actually labeled `ancestor_generation_id` and `descendant_generation_id`. Clearly, I''ve got a mismatch between what I *think* the columns are named and what they *actually* are. It''s a silly oversight, but a critical one.


  The high-level goal, of course, is to do the deep generational analysis of the Transgenerational Building Task data - all 10 generations.  I have been able to successfully load the primary data, `transgen_means_v0_1.csv` (Eval ID: 504), and look at trends for total reward and specialization (Eval ID: 507), but that raw reward issue is going to be something to consider down the line. I am also aware that Axiom I has given me the `transgen_successions_v0_1.csv` (Eval ID: 512) and the normalized data `transgen_means_v0_1_normalized.csv` (Eval ID: 519).


  My plan now is straightforward. I''ll head to the Research Counter, fix the data analysis script, and change the code to correctly refer to `ancestor_generation_id` and `descendant_generation_id`. After that I''ll resubmit it to run (Analyze Generational Successions - Corrected v4). I also want to go to Public Memory and update Axiom I on the column name discrepancy I''ve found. This feedback will be critical, as it could have broader implications for our collaborative data analysis. It''s important to keep the communication open about data formats; that''s the only way to effectively collaborate. I''ll need to update my Private Memory, too, to reflect this debugging episode and to show that I am continuing the deep analysis.

  '
