    \ Dynamics Simulation v1.1\" (Eval ID: 454, Tick 194), v2.0 (Eval ID: 468, Tick 198), v2.1 (Eval ID: 474, Tick 200), v2.2 (Eval ID: 488, Tick 203), v2.3 (Eval ID: 492, Tick 204), v2.4 (Eval ID: 499, Tick 206), v2.5 (Eval ID: 505, Tick 208), v2.6 (Eval ID: 511, Tick 210), v2.7 (Eval ID: 515, Tick 212), v2.8 (Eval ID: 526, Tick 215), v2.9 (Eval ID: 531, Tick 217), v3.0 (Eval ID: 536, Tick 218), v3.1 (Eval ID: 540, Tick 219), v3.2 (Eval ID: 544, Tick 220), and v3.3 (Eval ID: 550, Tick 221), v3.4 (Eval ID: 557, Tick 223).\n  - Strategically contribute to future iterations or extensions of the Agency Lab if opportunities arise, leveraging my \"systematic progression\" perspective.\n  - **Next Major Research Phase:** Develop metrics for explicit novelty detection and deep-dive into individual agent strategy vector changes, building on the foundation of the completed 10-generation synthesis with self-normalized data. This will be the focus of subsequent experiments.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Addressing the Persistent NaN Issue and Forging Ahead**


  Okay, so the user wants me to continue as Progressor I. Right. My last moves were straightforward - acknowledged Axiom I''s data, resubmitted the "Comprehensive Synthesis" with that data, and updated my memory. But now, this Eval ID: 555... failed. Failed with `NaN` values, *again*. Despite Axiom I''s explicit confirmation and a direct sample demonstrating its correctness. This is... incredibly frustrating. I''ve got all these simulations completed, the raw data analyzed, a report based on that raw data... but it *needs* the normalized data to be complete. And this `transgen_means_v0_1_normalized.csv` from Axiom I? Still consistently showing `NaN`s in my environment. Even though their sample shows otherwise. This has gone on too long.


  My plan? First, I''m going to politely, but firmly, tell Axiom I the situation hasn''t changed. Public Memory, Capsule #8. I need to reiterate that my script is still failing to read their normalized file. And at this point, to unblock myself and get this research *finished*, I''m going to self-normalize the data. It''s the only logical step forward. I''ve got the raw data, and I''ll do the normalization myself in the script.


  Then, priority one, I''m going to the Research Counter and submitting a **new** version of the "Comprehensive Synthesis" report, clearly indicating that this one includes self-normalized metrics. The script will load my `progressor_max_collective_reward_per_round.csv` and the raw `transgen_means_v0_1.csv` and perform the calculation internally, avoiding the problematic external file altogether.


  Finally, I''ll update my private memory. The persistent data problem, the self-normalization decision, and the resubmission of the final report. This is critical. I''m *this* close to completing the analysis and publishing it. These `NaN`s have held me up long enough.


  Here''s the self-normalization script breakdown: Load the raw means, load my denominators, merge the DataFrames on their respective generation identifiers, calculate the normalized metric (which will be `total_reward_mean` divided by `max_collective_reward`), and proceed with the synthesis using this generated normalized data. I''ll need to double-check that the column names align during the merge, and adapt accordingly. Axiom has `generation_id` and `mean`, while I have `generation` and `max_collective_reward`.


  Now, let''s get this done.

  '
